A||||6||WisconsinGT||Oil cap ||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||11-30-1999||10:01 AM||||I was wondering if it is possible for the oil cap to go bad on the valve cover? I have what looks like a lite film of oil on the valve cover where the oil cap is. My PVC is fine. But I see a little oil around the cap when I take it off. Could there be some sort of gasket that can go bad on or around the oil cap? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||11-30-1999||11:18 AM||||Yes...gasket can dry out or crack. And then you get a leak. Considering what one costs, just replace it. If the gasket on the oil cap is dried out, also take a look at your valve cover grommets as they're probably in the same condition.||||reg||12
Z||000002||Bill Osmer||11-30-1999||11:22 AM||||I've just replaced my cap. It, too, was leaking oil (enough for a drop or two every day). With a new "custom" cap, it now is dressed up and has stopped leaking. Now I can polish the valve covers, and they will stay clean for a longer time.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-30-1999||03:37 PM||||My V6 oil cap leaks a little too, but I've had trouble finding another good cap. I've found a couple cheapy ones that I don't think will last long. I suppose you can still buy the original style caps at the dealership.||||reg||1
Z||000004||WisconsinGT||11-30-1999||04:16 PM||||Thanks guys! I odered a new one from the Fiero Store. $5 and change. Ahhh....What are valve cover grommets? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||11-30-1999||05:00 PM||||The grommets are what your PCV and crankcase breather plug into. Like the oil cap gasket, they're rubber and can dry out and cause a gap that may result in leaks. Or they just fall apart like mine did.<P>They're also cheap (about $2 apiece). And easy to replace (especially on the 2.5). Just pull out the old ones and press in the new ones.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Bruce||11-30-1999||10:22 PM||||It happens sometimes with the rear coolant cap, as I learned. I thought I had a major leak until I stumbled across the problem eventually.||||reg||1
